  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nRidging begins to flatten today as the next coastal trough \nbegins to dive southward along the West Coast. Light north to \nnortheast winds behind yesterday's weak front and the decrease \nin the ridge will keep temperatures much closer to normal today,\nwith highs mainly in the upper 50s to mid 60s. A weakening \ntrough tucked in ahead of the larger west coast trough will \nmove into western Colorado this afternoon, spreading a few more \nclouds into southern WY. There may be just enough moisture to \nspark a shower or two, but surface humidity remains \nparticularly dry still, so likely will only see a few virga \nshowers this evening across Sweetwater County.\n\nMonday will see an increase in moisture, but with little to no\nupper level support, not expecting anything more than a few\nclouds across SW WY during the afternoon.\n\nTuesday with the trough stalling off the California Coast,\nsouthwest flow aloft will continue to bring some more Pacific \nmoisture into western WY. There may be enough diffluence aloft\nfor a few showers for the western mountain ranges during the \nafternoon, but wringing any moisture out of these will be a hard\ntask.\n\nThe coastal trough finally ejects northeast into the Rockies by\nThursday. Still varying differences in model solutions regarding\nwhere it finally goes, but in any case, there will be some\nprecipitation Thursday into Friday, mainly for areas west of the\nDivide.",
